{
  "_doc": "Loop configuration - the machine-readable knobs the driver (scripts/run_campaign.py --supervise) reads. Set at scaffold time by new-campaign from this campaign's unit cost (the WHY lives in roles/worker.md, 'How the loop runs'); human-editable mid-campaign - the driver logs any change it detects. Knob semantics: STRUCTURED_LOOP_PROPOSAL s4.1 rules 2-4.",
  "campaign": {
    "_doc": "What KIND of campaign this is - the property the noise-floor and comparison checks branch on. It is a SETUP-TIME decision, not a per-experiment skip: turning a control off because one result looks awkward is the failure the control exists to prevent. Changing it mid-campaign is legitimate only when the campaign's nature actually changed, and the change is visible in every later snapshot manifest (results/snapshots/<exp>/manifest.txt records it), never only here. (The metric's plausible range is not set here: it lives in the FROZEN scripts/metric.py as PLAUSIBILITY_BOUNDS.) noise_model: 'stochastic' (repeated runs differ; a win must clear the replicate spread) or 'deterministic' (repeated runs are identical, so the comparison is exact and the relevant test is a numerical tolerance, not a spread). noise_floor: the once-estimated replicate spread for a stochastic campaign (verify/SKILL.md, 'Clear the noise floor'), re-estimated only when something that could move it changes; null = not yet estimated, which leaves the noise-floor check unavailable rather than assumed-passed. tolerance: the numerical equality tolerance for a deterministic campaign; null = not yet set. metric_version / data_version: the comparability tags stamped onto every scoreboard row and every result JSON. A ranking claim is valid ONLY WITHIN one (metric_version, data_version) pair - when the FROZEN metric or the dataset changes, BUMP the tag here and re-score saved contenders under the new version before comparing. Never compare a number across versions. reproducible: whether one unit can be re-executed from its config alone (seeded; not a one-shot measurement, not hardware-in-the-loop) - a fact the verifier reads when it chooses how to refute a claim; it re-runs a search only when the claim is about the search.",
    "noise_model": "{{NOISE_MODEL}}",
    "noise_floor": {{NOISE_FLOOR}},
    "tolerance": {{METRIC_TOLERANCE}},
    "metric_version": "{{METRIC_VERSION}}",
    "data_version": "{{DATA_VERSION}}",
    "reproducible": true,
    "_doc_metric_revision": "metric_revision + metric_epochs: the FROZEN metric is epoched, not immutable. A revision of what it measures (kind 'semantics') or a fix that makes the specified metric run correctly (kind 'mechanics') opens a new metric EPOCH: bump metric_version, add an entry to metric_epochs keyed by that version - {kind, status, evidence, expected_direction, opened_by: 'worker@<exp_name>' | 'user'} - re-score retained contenders under the new ruler, and leave one line under 'Where we stand' in results/JOURNAL.md. metric_epochs exists only once a campaign has revised its metric: with no block, the current metric_version IS the co-designed first epoch (ratified). evidence: a semantics epoch cites the verify kill verdict against the old metric (results/verdicts/<id>.json); a mechanics epoch names the correctness check that reproduced the discrepancy. expected_direction: 'harder' | 'easier' | 'neutral' - what the revision does to the current best's score ('easier' is the goalpost-moving shape and needs a REFUTED-tier verdict). status: 'pending' until the user ratifies it, then 'ratified' or 'rolled-back' - standing never deletes a result; a rolled-back epoch's rows stay on the scoreboard, labelled by their metric_version. The block is append-only and hook-guarded; ratification is the user's, from an attended session. The runner binds each epoch's scorer hash in results/correctness_state.json on its first experiment and refuses to spend on a change to the scorer's code that no epoch declares (comments, docstrings and formatting do not count). metric_revision: WHO may open an epoch. 'cautious' (default): any revision the loop's agents want raises results/ESCALATE.md and the loop waits for the user. 'autonomous': the agents may enact a VERIFIED revision themselves as a pending epoch and continue; the user ratifies after. 'semi-autonomous' is reserved (not yet defined) and is refused. The dial is the USER's decision: it is changed from an attended session (edit this file, ask run-campaign, or `run_campaign.py --set-metric-revision`), never by a driver-spawned role - the config guard refuses that - and the driver logs every change it detects and stamps the governing value into every snapshot manifest.",
    "metric_revision": "{{METRIC_REVISION}}"
  },
  "unit_cost": {
    "_doc": "What one experiment costs. typical_wall_clock_s sets the loop knobs below; typical_disk_mb is what one unit ADDS to results/ (save-set + snapshot + figures + any bulk data), estimated at scaffold time from the artifact plan - the driver refuses to launch, and halts at the loop top, when free disk falls below 3 units' worth. 0 disables that check.",
    "class": "{{UNIT_COST_CLASS}}",
    "typical_wall_clock_s": {{UNIT_COST_S}},
    "typical_disk_mb": {{UNIT_COST_DISK_MB}}
  },
  "verifier": {
    "_doc": "The independent-check agents the driver spawns (the verifier the preset owes a unit; the claim-audit capstone at the end): model / claude_effort apply only to Claude; codex_model / codex_reasoning_effort apply only to Codex. A blank model or effort inherits that harness's configured default. (An older loop.json's 'planner' block is read for these when this block is absent - the planner itself is retired,.)",
    "model": "{{VERIFIER_MODEL}}",
    "claude_effort": "{{VERIFIER_CLAUDE_EFFORT}}",
    "codex_model": "{{VERIFIER_CODEX_MODEL}}",
    "codex_reasoning_effort": "{{VERIFIER_CODEX_REASONING_EFFORT}}"
  },
  "worker": {
    "_doc": "Fresh-context iteration agent, one unit per spawn, which the worker chooses from the JOURNAL (no queue); it ends the campaign by writing NEXT: STOP. Long solver runs execute under the driver or the worker's shell - NEVER inside a background subagent (the runner's results/.run_heartbeat is the driver's dead-worker signal, and a backgrounded solve loses it). model / claude_effort apply only to Claude; codex_model / codex_reasoning_effort apply only to Codex. A blank model or effort inherits that harness's configured default. iteration_timeout_hours = the absolute cap; inactivity_timeout_min = kill a worker with no output AND no heartbeat change for this long.",
    "model": "{{WORKER_MODEL}}",
    "claude_effort": "{{WORKER_CLAUDE_EFFORT}}",
    "codex_model": "{{WORKER_CODEX_MODEL}}",
    "codex_reasoning_effort": "{{WORKER_CODEX_REASONING_EFFORT}}",
    "iteration_timeout_hours": {{ITERATION_TIMEOUT_HOURS}},
    "retries": 1,
    "inactivity_timeout_min": 30
  },
  "driver": {
    "_doc": "Supervisor-driver knobs. All code-defaulted - an older loop.json without this section keeps working. backend: auto|claude|codex|custom; new-campaign records the first launch harness here, and --backend may override it when switching. max_consecutive_failures: N operational (unit-scoped) failures in a row = systemic -> hard halt; below N the driver shelves the unit (kind=operational) and continues. verifier_retries: extra attempts after a verifier spawn returns without its artifact (a verifier that exits ok having written no verdict is the usual case - a refuter launched in the background and abandoned - and is retried with a note naming what is missing). stream: auto|on|off - auto adds the stream-json live feed only for the default claude CLI; Codex always keeps its JSONL protocol active, while off suppresses item-level feed lines. inbox_max_chars caps the per-spawn USER-NOTES injection from results/inbox/. heartbeat_stale_min / detached_grace_min govern the post-exit wait for a detached runner that is still solving: the wait ends as soon as the heartbeat stops changing for two runner ticks (or heartbeat_stale_min if shorter), detached_grace_min being only the outer bound. permission_mode: the --permission-mode spawned agents run under (default 'auto', the standard classifier: allows local file ops in project scope, reads, and killing a subprocess THIS session created by its tracked PID; cleanly denies - no prompt, verified headless - killing a process it did not create, secret writes, exfiltration, and spawning bypass agents). Set 'bypassPermissions' for a campaign that needs a soft-denied action (irreversible overwrite of a pre-existing untracked file, a cross-session kill, a force-push). Env override AUTORESEARCH_PERMISSION_MODE; only applied to the default claude CLI. skill_listing: names|full - names (default) makes every spawn list ALL installed skills by name only (the campaign files name the skill to use; the descriptions cost ~7k tokens per turn on a 1M-context model), which also hides other plugins' descriptions from the spawn; full keeps the harness's own listing. Interactive sessions in the campaign folder are never affected. Env override AUTORESEARCH_SKILL_LISTING; only applied to the default claude CLI. auto_memory: off|on - off (default) turns the harness's per-directory auto-memory off inside spawns, so nothing passes between workers outside the campaign's record (a note worth passing on goes in the JOURNAL's Traps and tooling); on keeps it. Env override AUTORESEARCH_AUTO_MEMORY. mcp_servers: none|inherit|<path> - none (default) gives spawns no MCP server at all (not the user's, not a project .mcp.json, not the claude.ai account's connectors); inherit loads whatever the harness would; a campaign-relative path loads exactly the servers in that MCP config file (e.g. an instrument driven through MCP). Env override AUTORESEARCH_MCP_SERVERS. Both only applied to the default claude CLI; interactive sessions in the campaign folder are never affected. limit_wait_max_min: a spawn the harness kills in its first turn within seconds (ok=False turns=1 dur=1s), or whose result names a usage/rate limit, is the model's usage limit, not the unit - the driver waits (doubling from 5 min) up to this many minutes in total and relaunches, spending no retry and never counting it toward SYSTEMIC; 0 = treat such deaths as ordinary failures.",
    "backend": "{{AGENT_BACKEND}}",
    "max_consecutive_failures": 3,
    "verifier_retries": 1,
    "stream": "auto",
    "permission_mode": "auto",
    "skill_listing": "names",
    "auto_memory": "off",
    "mcp_servers": "none",
    "inbox_max_chars": 20000,
    "feed_line_chars": 160,
    "heartbeat_stale_min": 5,
    "detached_grace_min": 60,
    "limit_wait_max_min": 90
  },
  "verify": {
    "_doc": "How much verification this campaign buys, and of what kind - ONE choice, preset: none | minimal | light | heavy | intensive (the standardized descriptions are the physkit plugin's docs/reference/verification-presets.md; the runner prints the resolved table at preflight and stamps it into every results/checks_<NN>.json). Every result gets at most ONE check, the strongest its preset owes it, and its label says which ran. none: nothing owed - the worker checks what it judges worth checking; no claim audit. minimal (the default): self-check every unit; self-verify (the verify protocol, in the worker's own session) a result outside the metric's PLAUSIBLE RANGE (metric.PLAUSIBILITY_BOUNDS - a probable implementation error, so rarely). light: as minimal, and a new best is checked by the NEXT worker (a peer check) once the next unit has not beaten it. heavy: self-verify every result that is not a new best; a new best gets a driver-spawned verifier once it survives one unit. intensive: a blinded driver-spawned verifier on every result. Under every preset, a worker that CONTESTS a gate outcome is owed an independent verifier instead (roles/worker.md). The claim audit at the end (every preset but none) re-checks the final best. Whatever the preset: the runner floor (scorer selftest, the checks artifact, ledger and epoch gates) always runs; derived numbers are ALWAYS recomputed from raw artifacts. Changing the preset mid-campaign is legitimate and logged by the driver. strategy: auto preserves the nested verifier for Claude/custom and uses one fresh driver-direct refuter turn for Codex; legacy-nested forces the nested path; driver-direct is Codex-only.",
    "preset": "{{VERIFICATION_PRESET}}",
    "strategy": "auto"
  }
}
